{% youtube src="https://youtu.be/o-6jb78uuP0" title="Remote caching with Nx Cloud" /%}

Rebuilding and retesting the same code repeatedly is costly. Nx offers a sophisticated and battle-tested computation caching system that ensures **code is never rebuilt twice**. This:

- drastically **speeds up your task execution times** while developing locally and even more [in CI](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache)
- **saves you money on CI/CD costs** by reducing the number of tasks that need to be executed

Nx **restores both the terminal output and the files** created from running the task (e.g., your build or dist directory).

## How caching works

Before running a cacheable task, Nx calculates a hash from the task's inputs.
Two runs with the same hash represent the same computation, so Nx can reuse the earlier result.
A task hash can include:

- Project source files and files from project dependencies.
- Relevant workspace configuration.
- Versions of external dependencies.
- Runtime values, such as the operating system and CPU architecture.
- Command-line arguments passed to the task.

![Inputs used to calculate a task hash](../../../assets/concepts/caching/nx-hashing.svg)

Nx checks the local cache for the hash, then the [remote cache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) when one is configured.
On a cache hit, Nx restores the cached output files and prints the stored terminal output.
On a cache miss, Nx runs the task and stores the result locally, and in the remote cache when the current authentication settings permit writes.

![Nx checking local and remote caches](../../../assets/concepts/caching/cache.svg)

Nx hashes each task in the task graph separately, so some tasks can be cache hits while others run.
Commands that create the same task with the same arguments produce the same hash: `nx build myapp` and `nx run-many -t build -p myapp` share a cache entry, while `nx build myapp --sourcemap` doesn't.

![A large task graph with cached tasks](../../../assets/concepts/caching/task-graph-big.svg)

### What Nx caches

Nx caches task results at the process level, whatever tool the task runs.
A cache entry contains:

- Terminal output written to standard output and standard error.
- Files and directories matched by the target's [`outputs`](/docs/kb/configure-outputs).
- The task hash that identifies the entry.

Source files and other inputs aren't stored, only their hashes.
To change what a task hashes or stores, see [configure inputs and outputs](#configure-inputs-and-outputs).

## Define cacheable tasks

To enable caching for `build` and `test`, edit the `targetDefaults` property in `nx.json` to include entries for the `build` and `test` tasks:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "targetDefaults": {
    "build": {
      "cache": true
    },
    "test": {
      "cache": true
    }
  }
}
```

{% aside type="note" title="Cacheable operations need to be side effect free" %}
This means that given the same input they should always result in
the same output. As an example, e2e test runs that hit the backend API cannot be cached as the backend might influence
the result of the test run.
{% /aside %}

## Enable remote caching

By default, Nx caches task results locally. The biggest benefit of caching comes from using remote caching in CI, where you can **share the cache between different runs**. Nx comes with a managed remote caching solution built on top of Nx Cloud.

To enable remote caching, connect your workspace to [Nx Cloud](https://nx.dev/nx-cloud?utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=cache-task-results) by running the following command:

```shell
npx nx@latest connect
```

{% call_to_action title="Connect from your browser" url="https://cloud.nx.app/get-started?utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=nx-cloud-signup&utm_content=cache-task-results" icon="nxcloud" description="Sign in to Nx Cloud and connect your repository without the CLI" /%}

Learn more about [remote caching with Nx Cloud](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache).

## Configure inputs and outputs

Inputs decide what goes into a task's hash, such as files, environment variables, and external dependency versions.
Outputs are the files Nx stores with the terminal output and restores on a cache hit.
How much you configure depends on where the task comes from.

[Inferred tasks](/docs/features/run-tasks#inferred-tasks) get their inputs and outputs from the plugin, which reads them from tool configuration such as `vite.config.ts`.
Change them only when the plugin's settings don't match what the task does.

Explicit tasks, such as `package.json` scripts and `project.json` targets, start from the Nx defaults.
Every file in the project and in the projects it depends on is an input.
Only `build` and `prepare` targets get default outputs (`{workspaceRoot}/dist/{projectRoot}`, `{projectRoot}/dist`, `{projectRoot}/build`, and `{projectRoot}/public`).
A `test` script that writes a coverage report caches only its terminal output until you list the report directory in `outputs`.
Narrow the inputs too, so edits to unrelated files don't cause cache misses.

Set inputs and outputs for every matching target in `nx.json`, or for one project in `project.json` or `package.json`.
This example keeps Markdown edits from invalidating `build` and stores the build output in `dist/<project-name>` at the workspace root:

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Globally" %}

```json
// nx.json
{
  "targetDefaults": {
    "build": {
      "inputs": ["{projectRoot}/**/*", "!{projectRoot}/**/*.md"],
      "outputs": ["{workspaceRoot}/dist/{projectName}"]
    }
  }
}
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Project Level (project.json)" %}

```json
// packages/some-project/project.json
{
  "name": "some-project",
  "targets": {
    "build": {
      ...
      "inputs": ["!{projectRoot}/**/*.md"],
      "outputs": ["{workspaceRoot}/dist/apps/some-project"],
      ...
    }
    ...
  }
}
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Project Level (package.json)" %}

```json
// packages/some-project/package.json
{
  "name": "some-project",
  "nx": {
    "targets": {
      "build": {
        ...
        "inputs": ["!{projectRoot}/**/*.md"],
        "outputs": ["{workspaceRoot}/dist/apps/some-project"],
        ...
      }
      ...
    }
  }
}
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

Learn more [about configuring inputs including `namedInputs`](/docs/kb/configure-inputs).

## Configure caching automatically with Ultracache

On Nx Cloud, [Ultracache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache#ultracache) traces the files a task reads when it runs on Workers.
Later CI runs hash the task from those files in place of its declared file inputs, so a change to a file the task never reads no longer causes a cache miss.

Ultracache is on for every task that runs on Workers, with nothing to configure, and needs Nx 22.6 or later.
Declared `outputs` still decide which files Nx stores and restores, and runs outside CI hash from declared `inputs`.
To set it up, [connect your workspace to Nx Cloud](#enable-remote-caching) and [distribute tasks on Workers](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution).

## Troubleshoot cache settings

Caching is hard. If you run into issues, check out the following resources:

- [Debug cache misses](/docs/kb/troubleshoot-cache-misses)
- [Turn off or skip the cache](/docs/kb/skipping-cache)
- [Change the cache location](/docs/kb/change-cache-location)
- [Clear the local or remote cache](/docs/reference/nx-commands#nx-reset)
